# ADVERSARIAL FIXTURE — hostile TEXT in `description=`, the key written FOR
# the machine reader.
#
# `description=` is the one string in the language whose declared audience is
# a program rather than a person (case 808): it puts no ink on the page and
# becomes an SVG `<title>` tooltip. That makes it the surface where a
# sanitising engine is least likely to be noticed doing it — nobody looks at
# a tooltip — and the surface a host is most likely to lift back out of the
# artifact and re-emit somewhere else. So it gets its own fixture.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS. Unlike `note=`, `description=` IS projected into
# the model, per field. The `.model.json` golden therefore holds the hostile
# strings BYTE FOR BYTE, and that is the assertion: the model carries the
# author's text, not a cleaned-up version of it. An engine that escaped on
# the way in, or stripped angle brackets, or dropped the value and kept the
# key, all fail this golden.
#
# The complement — that the same strings reach the artifact as ESCAPED TEXT
# and never as a `<title>` element containing markup — is `gate:safesvg`'s,
# which renders this file and reads the output structurally (core §15).
figdown 0.3 bitfield
title "<script>alert(1)</script>"

bitfield hdr "]]></metadata><script>alert(1)</script><metadata>" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "Version" 4 description="<script>alert(1)</script>"
field "Flags" 12 description="\" onload=\"alert(1)"
field "Len" 8 description="javascript:alert(1) url(https://example.invalid/x) @import"
field "Rsvd" 8 description="&amp; & < > ]]> <![CDATA[ </metadata> \\ done"
